            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

From the blower door test, your house has fairly high air leakage. I was not able to use the infrared camera but did locate some locations using other techniques. 



I recommend outfitting electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. I did notice quite a bit of air leakage through the outlets I investigated. 



I was not able to check air leakage around your basement door, but as it is an older door I suspect it is a source of air leakage. I recommend checking the weather stripping around this door or replacing this door entirely. 



I was not able to check for air leakage in your crawlspace, but I suspect that this is a significant location for air leakage. 



I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit. 



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $210 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 6.7 GJ. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home.



Savings:

By adding R15 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $600 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 18.8 GJ, and qualify for up to$2,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors>

The exposed floors of your home appear to be 2x10 joists with 8 inches (R28) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation, and increasing the fiberglass batt insulation to R32 for a total R value of approximately R47.



Savings:

By increasing the floor insulation in both exposed floor sections to R47, you could potentially save $110 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate your entire exposed floor and a minimum floor area of 14 square metres (150 square feet) must be insulated. You must also increase the insulation value by a minimum of RSI 3.5 (R-20). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</exposedFloors>
                <foundation>

Your crawlspace walls appear to be 2x4 framing with 2 6 inch thick fibreglass batts held in place with plastic for a total R-value of R28. I have modelled replacing this insulation with spray foam insulation. This will not increase the overall insulation value in your crawlspace, but may reduce the air leakage in this part of your home. 



I have also modelled increasing the insulation in your basement storage. Your mechanical room is currently only partially insulated. I recommend that you increase the interior wall insulation from R12 to R20, and add 3 inches of rigid board insulation to the pony wall and the exterior of the concrete that is not below ground.



Savings:

By replacing the insulation in your crawlspace and increasing the insulation in your basement as described above, you could potentially save $960 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 29.8 GJ. You are not eligible for any rebates for replacing the crawlspace insulation as you already have R28 in the walls, but you may be eligible for a rebate of up to $778 for insulation in the basement.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate for the crawlspace, you must insulate 100 percent of the crawl space wall area, including the header area, and increase its insulation value by a minimum of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate for the basement storage area, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <windows>

All of your windows are older double pane windows that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass. Overall they are in reasonable shape. For information, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all the windows and the two sets of patio doors with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. We recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Savings: 

By replacing all the windows and the two sets of patio doors with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $445 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 13.8 GJ.  



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <doors>

As your back door appears to be an uninsulated solid wood door, I have modelled replacing it with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified patio door rated for climate zone 3.



Savings:

By replacing your back door with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified door rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $60 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.0 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



</doors>
                <AdditionalComments>

ECM PUMPS

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house, you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



PELLET STOVE

As you are interested in changing your wood stove to a pellet stove, I did run some quick numbers for you. Using your current wood stove to meet 10% of your heating load will save you about $190 per year in energy costs, assuming you are purchasing wood. If you change to a pellet stove and use it to meet 10% of your heating load, you will save about $310 per year in energy costs. 



If you are harvesting your own wood, the wood stove you currently have may be the cheaper option. However, many people choose pellet stoves because of the convenience. 



If you purchase a qualified pellet stove, you will qualify for up to 1/2 of pre-tax purchase cost of the stove to a maximum of $1,000, from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



While in your attic, I noted that the vent ducting for one of your bathroom fans is not connected. This should be be fixed promptly as this vent is dumping warm moist air into your attic space, and if the moisture in this air condenses on the cold surfaces in your attic it puts your home at risk for water damage.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Alexandra Giroux

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: alexandra.giroux@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
